  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn approaching cold front will trigger scattered showers and \nthunderstorms Thursday afternoon across the area. A Marginal \nrisk of severe thunderstorms is outlooked by the Storm \nPrediction Center, with low-end damaging wind and hail risks. \nInstability will be sufficient for thunderstorm development, \nwith models outputting anywhere from 1,000 to 2,000 J/kg of \nMLCAPE Thursday afternoon. 0-6 km bulk shear is only expected to\nreach 20-25 kts Thursday, which will be the limiting factor. \nThis is why the Storm Prediction Center has capped the threat \nat Marginal for now.\n\nThe threat of excessive rainfall will be Marginal across much of \nWest Virginia and southwest Virginia. Precipitable Water Values will \nbe highest right along the cold front at 1.70 to 1.90 inches. \nOtherwise, this doesn't look like a widespread flash flooding \nthreat. Northern West Virginia and southeast Ohio still remain \nsensitive after the heavy rainfall seen over the past several weeks, \nand one-hour Flash Flood guidance remains lower in these areas at \n1.30 to 1.50 inches. Slow-moving or training thunderstorms that \nimpact these sensitive areas may be at risk of flash flooding \nThursday. Otherwise, expect a lower-end flood risk across low-lying \nand urban areas.\n\nMesoscale models suggest thunderstorm activity should diminish \nbetween 8:00 PM and 10:00 PM local time with the loss of peak \nheating.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\n500-mb heights will begin rising Friday as a broad, upper-level area \nof high pressure migrates from the southern Plains to the mid-\nAtlantic region. This will bring dry, but increasingly hot weather \nto the middle Ohio Valley and central Appalachians from Friday \nthrough Tuesday, with high temperatures expected to reach the \nlower 90s across the lowlands beginning Sunday. The ridge is \nexpected to break down by midweek, which will allow for showers \nand thunderstorms to return by Wednesday or Thursday.",
